lts; staticFilter (optional boolean; same ISymbol.IsStatic test as get_symbol_info / get_document_outline / search_symbols / find_callers / find_references / get_diagnostics staticFilter) keeps only static (true) or only instance (false) implementations, e.g. static implementations of a static abstract interface member; it judges each returned implementation symbol itself, unlike find_references / find_callers / get_diagnostics staticFilter, which judge the enclosing site symbol of each usage / call / diagnostic; implementing types are never static, so for type targets true returns an empty success while false or omitted keeps every implementing type; an implementing member's staticness always equals its target's, so for a member target the filter is all-or-nothing (the target's staticness decides); explicit interface implementations (static or instance) are judged on their own IsStatic; a default interface member re-implemented explicitly in a derived interface is returned and judged on that re-implementation (the derived interface itself is not returned for a type target); records, record structs, structs, nested types, partial members (reported once, at the defining declaration), generic implementers, and implementers of a self-referencing generic static abstract member (ISelf<TSelf>) are judged like any other; implementations in other projects of the solution are returned and filtered; implementations declared only in metadata are never listed (for a metadata target they still count toward totalCount, so truncated can be true); current behavior: static-class targets, abstract or virtual class targets, and abstract / virtual class members with overrides (including a generic base such as GB<T>.GM) return no implementations even without a filter; combined with the other filters and applied before maxResults; a --static-filter value other than true/false (case-insensitive) is rejected with an error (the MCP tool reports INTERNAL_ERROR); sourceFile still locates the target symbol");
