or Field; case-insensitive; same values and parsing as search-symbols / find-references / find-callers kind-filter) restricts to diagnostics whose diagnostic site — the same site accessibilityFilter / namespaceFilter resolve (declaration-attached members such as CS0169 on a field; otherwise the enclosing member / type, with lambdas / local functions looked through; methods / constructors / accessors → Method; fields → Field or Constant; namespace-level sites and diagnostics with no source location never match) — has that kind via SymbolKindMapper, combined with the other filters and applied before maxResults (invalid → InvalidSymbolKind); nameFilter (optional: ordinal, case-insensitive substring; same matching as find-references / find-callers name-filter) restricts to diagnostics whose diagnostic site — the same site accessibilityFilter / kindFilter judge — has a simple name (ISymbol.Name: accessors get_X / set_X, constructors .ctor) containing it (namespace-level sites and diagnostics with no source location never match; a diagnostic inside a delegate's synthesized Invoke / BeginInvoke / EndInvoke / .ctor site resolves to the delegate itself, so nameFilter matches the delegate's name and filters like Invoke or .ctor never match such a site; matching is a plain ordinal, case-insensitive Contains, so wildcards such as * and ? are not supported and are matched literally), combined with the other filters and applied before maxResults; staticFilter (optional boolean, --static-filter) keeps only diagnostics whose diagnostic site — the same site accessibilityFilter / kindFilter / nameFilter judge — is static when true (ISymbol.IsStatic: static methods, static constructors, operators, static properties / events and their accessors, static and const fields — Roslyn reports const as static — static classes, and members of a static class) or instance when false (optional; omit = no static filter). Edge rules: unlike find-references, a declaration-attached diagnostic is judged on the member or type it is attached to, not the containing type — a member's return or parameter type (public static MissingType Make()), an attribute on a member ([Missing] public static void M()) or on a type ([Missing] static class Util), and a field, const, or auto-property initializer (static int S = Missing;, static int P { get; } = Missing;) follow that member's or type's own staticness, as does the body of an expression-bodied property (static int P => Missing;, judged on its static getter); likewise unlike find-references, a top-level type's base list (class B1 : MissingBase is kept by false) and its type-parameter constraints are judged on the type (find-references treats both as namespace-level and never matches them), a method's type-parameter constraints are judged on the method, and doc-comment diagnostics (CS1574 unresolved cref, CS1591 missing XML comment) are judged on the documented member or type (find-references judges a member's cref on the containing type); lambdas and local functions, including static lambdas, static local functions, and a non-static local function inside a static method, are looked through to the declaring member and judged on its staticness (unlike find-callers, which judges a local function as its own calling symbol); a finalizer, an indexer (C# has no static indexers), and a delegate type (including a diagnostic in its signature or synthesized Invoke / BeginInvoke / EndInvoke / .ctor) are instance; a custom event accessor follows the event; an extension method and a static interface member are static, a default interface member is instance; members of a nested type are judged on their own staticness, not the containing type's, so instance members of a class nested in a static class are kept by false; records, record structs, and structs are judged like classes. Parser / missing-token errors are judged on the declaration containing the missing token (CS1002 on the } of static void M() { int x = 1 } is kept by true). Enum